{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "_comment": "City data for Sister Cities (spec #2): the pool the agent suggests from when a player joins, and the lookup table used to reassign a duplicate city pick to a geographically close alternative. Players may still pick ANY city, in or out of this file (spec #2 -- 'the agent suggests possibilities but players may pick freely'); off-gazetteer picks are handled by resolution_rules.off_gazetteer below. Behavioural knobs (uniqueness enforcement, how many suggestions to offer, search radius) live in config.json -> cities, not here. This file is data only -- M1 ships no runtime logic.",

  "coordinate_note": "lat/lon are approximate city-centre values to one decimal degree. They exist to break ties when a duplicate's listed neighbours are all taken, not to survey anything.",

  "resolution_rules": {
    "_comment": "The procedure a later milestone's engine must implement. Written here so the data and the rule that consumes it stay together and neither gets re-derived elsewhere.",
    "normalization": [
      "Case-fold, trim, strip diacritics, and collapse internal whitespace before comparing.",
      "Match against name and every entry in aliases.",
      "Two picks that normalize to the same key are a duplicate, even if typed differently ('NYC' vs 'New York City').",
      "A parenthetical qualifier is part of the key, not decoration: 'Athens (Georgia)' and 'Athens' are different keys. If a player's pick matches a qualified name once the qualifier is dropped, ask which one they mean rather than guessing -- that is exactly the case where guessing puts a mayor on the wrong continent."
    ],
    "naming_convention": [
      "Every name in this file -- in name, aliases and nearby[] alike -- must be unambiguous ON ITS OWN, because nearby[] entries are handed to a player as a reassignment without any surrounding context. A bare toponym shared by two real cities gets a parenthetical qualifier: 'Athens (Georgia)', 'Toledo (Ohio)', 'San Antonio (Chile)', 'Vancouver (WA)'.",
      "The test a new entry must pass: no nearby[] string may normalize to the name or alias of a gazetteer city that is not, in fact, near the city listing it. An unqualified 'Athens' under Atlanta reassigns a Georgian mayor to Greece, which fails spec #2's 'geographically close' requirement while looking perfectly well-formed.",
      "Qualify the toponym even when nothing in this file currently collides with it -- the collision arrives with the next city added, and by then the bad reassignment is data, not a typo."
    ],
    "duplicate_reassignment": [
      "The player who claimed the city FIRST keeps it. The later pick is the one reassigned (spec #2: never silently allowed to collide).",
      "Walk the claimed city's nearby[] in listed order and take the first entry not already claimed by another city in this game.",
      "If every nearby[] entry is taken, take the nearest unclaimed gazetteer city in the same region by lat/lon, within config.cities.max_reassignment_search_radius_km.",
      "If that also fails, offer the reassigned player a free re-pick rather than assigning something absurd.",
      "Always tell the player what happened and why -- reassignment is announced, never silent."
    ],
    "off_gazetteer": [
      "A pick not in this file is accepted as-is and recorded in the game's runtime city list, which is what the uniqueness check actually reads (this file seeds it, it is not the ledger).",
      "If an off-gazetteer pick collides, the agent proposes candidates using the same nearest-first rule from its own geographic knowledge, and records the chosen alternative in the runtime list.",
      "nearby[] entries are themselves valid picks even though most have no entry of their own here."
    ],
    "suggestions": [
      "On join, offer config.cities.suggestions_offered_on_join cities drawn from entries with suggest_on_join = true, spread across regions, excluding anything already claimed.",
      "Suggestions are a prompt, not a menu -- always state that any city is allowed."
    ]
  },

